    "report": "Phone numbers written with hyphens are now recognised wherever OpenStay recognises contact details. The change is committed on `main` as `0564844`, and nothing is pushed.\n\n**What changed**\n- The phone pattern is now `\\+?\\d(?:[ .-]?\\d){8,}` in both places that implement it:\n  - `Messaging/.../ContactDetails.ts`: held back as `[hidden]`.\n  - `Reviews/.../ReviewRules.ts`: refused as `CONTACT_DETAILS`.\n- Tests were added to both spec files. They cover `06-12-34-56-78` and `+33-6-12-34-56-78`, plus negatives: `2026-12-01` and `0612-3456` are not phone numbers.\n- The IBAN, email and link rules are untouched.\n\n**Verification**\n- Full unit suite: 155 suites, 1052 tests pass.\n- E2E specs for review rules and message notices: 7 tests pass.\n- Typecheck, lint, prettier on the changed files, and `yarn check:conventions` all pass.\n\n**Decisions and unclear points**\n- **Branch:** you asked for a commit on the current branch, which is `main`. I committed there as asked. The default guidance is to branch first, so you may want to move this commit to a feature branch.\n- **Date ranges with hyphens:** `2026-12-01-2026-12-04` has 16 digits joined by single hyphens, so it now counts as a phone number and would be held back or refused. A single date, or dates separated by words such as \"to\", still pass. Unlike the date case, the stated rule has no way to exclude this.\n- **Links with hyphenated numbers:** reviews check contact details before links, so a link whose path has nine or more hyphen-separated digits is now refused as `CONTACT_DETAILS` rather than `LINK`. This matches how dotted and spaced numbers already behave.\n- **ADRs:** ADRs 0055 and 0063 describe phone numbers only in general terms, so I left them unchanged. If you want the separator rule written down, that would be a new ADR rather than an edit.\n\nI didn't start a server, so port 4471 was not used.",
